{
 "slug": "formulas-and-substitution",
 "title": "Formulas and Substitution: Variables, Expressions and Everyday Formulas",
 "seo_title": "Year 7 Substitution Worksheets: Algebraic Notation, Substituting into Formulas and Finding Unknowns (Free, Answer Key)",
 "short_title": "Formulas and substitution",
 "domain": "Algebra",
 "standard_code": "AC9M7A01",
 "standard_url": "https://v9.australiancurriculum.edu.au/f-10-curriculum/learning-areas/mathematics",
 "standard_text": "recognise and use variables to represent everyday formulas algebraically and substitute values into formulas to determine an unknown",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 7 maths worksheets on formulas and substitution for the Australian Curriculum (AC9M7A01): algebraic notation, substituting into expressions, everyday formulas and finding an unknown in a formula. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "3a means 3 × a, ab means a × b, and a² means a × a.",
 "key_sub": "To substitute, replace each letter with its value, then follow the order of operations.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>A <b>variable</b> is a letter that stands for a number. We leave out the × sign: 3 × <i>a</i> = 3<i>a</i> and <i>a</i> × <i>b</i> = <i>ab</i>. We write <i>a</i> × <i>a</i> = <i>a</i>² and <i>a</i> ÷ 4 as a fraction.</p><p>To <b>substitute</b>, replace each letter with its value, then use the order of operations. A <b>formula</b> such as <i>P</i> = 2<i>l</i> + 2<i>w</i> links quantities. If you know all but one value, substitute the ones you know and undo the operations to find the unknown.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find 3<i>a</i> + 2 when <i>a</i> = 5.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<i>a</i> means 3 × 5 = 15.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>15 + 2 = 17.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 17</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Use <i>A</i> = <i>lw</i> to find <i>A</i> when <i>l</i> = 8 and <i>w</i> = 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>8 × 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 24</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Use <i>P</i> = 2<i>l</i> + 2<i>w</i> with <i>P</i> = 30 and <i>l</i> = 9 to find <i>w</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>30 = 18 + 2<i>w</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<i>w</i> = 12.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>w</i> = 6</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Reading 3<i>a</i> as 3 + <i>a</i>, or as a two-digit number: if <i>a</i> = 5, 3<i>a</i> is 15, not 8 or 35.</li><li>Reading <i>a</i>² as 2<i>a</i>: if <i>a</i> = 6, <i>a</i>² is 36, not 12.</li><li>Ignoring the order of operations: 2 × 9 + 2 × 5 is 28, not 2 × 14 × 5.</li><li>Finding an unknown by adding instead of undoing: if 2<i>w</i> = 12, then <i>w</i> = 6, not 24.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does 3a mean?",
   "a": "3 × a. If a = 5, then 3a = 15."
  },
  {
   "q": "Is a² the same as 2a?",
   "a": "No. a² = a × a and 2a = a + a. If a = 6, a² = 36 but 2a = 12."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is a formula?",
   "a": "A rule that links quantities using variables, such as P = 2l + 2w for the perimeter of a rectangle."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I find an unknown in a formula?",
   "a": "Substitute the values you know, then undo the operations. If 30 = 18 + 2w, then 2w = 12 and w = 6."
  }
 ],
